I wrote the test suite in `packages/shared/test/build-commit.test.ts`. `npx tsc -p tsconfig.test.json` reports no errors, and running the files gives 22 passing and 38 failing tests.

**Why 22 already pass:** the interface file already contains working code for the constants and schemas: `UNKNOWN_BUILD`, `buildInfoSchema`, `versionResponseSchema` and the frozen `unknownBuildInfo`. Only the five functions throw `not implemented`. So every test for the schemas and the frozen object passes now, and every test that calls `formatVersion`, `isIdentified`, `commitUrl`, `sameBuild` or `buildBanner` fails, as expected.

**Coverage:** each of the 30 scenarios (11 happy, 7 unhappy, 12 boundary) has exactly two tests: one for the happy path, which runs through all of a Scenario Outline's examples, and one boundary case. Every boundary case either restates the spec or follows directly from it. Some examples:
- a version with surrounding spaces is kept as given, since values aren't trimmed;
- a 64-character SHA-256 commit is rejected;
- `repo.git.git` loses only one `.git` in `commitUrl`;
- `sameBuild` is false even when the same object with an unknown commit is passed twice;
- the banner still shows the build time when both version and commit are unknown.

**Conventions:** the file follows `schemas.test.ts`: `node:test` `test` and `node:assert/strict`, with flat top-level tests and no `describe`. Every test name starts with its scenario key, such as `[boundary-6]`. There is no clock, randomness, network or filesystem use. Only that one file was added; nothing else was changed.

60 tests, in 1 file.

