The hidden suite is in `packages/shared/test/hidden-build-commit/`: `schema.test.ts` and `functions.test.ts`. `npx tsc -p tsconfig.test.json` reports no errors. Run against the code as it stands, all 51 tests fail. Run against a reference implementation I built in the scratchpad, all 51 pass, along with the public suite and `schemas.test.ts`. Nothing in the repository outside that folder was changed.

**One choice to check:** the seam already fully implements `UNKNOWN_BUILD`, `unknownBuildInfo`, `buildInfoSchema` and `versionResponseSchema`. Tests that imported them straight from `build-info.js` would pass now, which breaks the rule that every test must fail first. So the tests for the constant and schema scenarios (`happy-1` to `happy-5`, `boundary-1`, `boundary-2`, `unhappy-1` to `unhappy-4`) read those exports through `../../src/index.js`. Each one first checks that it is the same object `build-info.js` exports. The spec requires `index.ts` to re-export the module, and the public suite never checks that. The catch is that an implementation that leaves out the re-export fails 20 tests under schema keys, even though the schemas themselves are correct. The function tests import from `../../src/build-info.js` directly.

**Shortcuts I checked:** each of these passed the public suite and failed the hidden one:
- removing `.git` anywhere in the repository URL, not just at the end (`boundary-6`);
- removing trailing slashes again after `.git` (`boundary-6`);
- removing at most two trailing slashes (`boundary-6`);
- trimming only spaces from the banner name, not tabs or newlines (`happy-11`, `boundary-10`, `unhappy-7`);
- treating commits as equal when they match on part of the SHA (`happy-10`);
- not re-exporting from `index.ts` (20 tests).

One partial-match shortcut, comparing only the first 7 characters and the last one, at first passed both suites. I added a commit that differs from SHA by one character in the middle, and the hidden suite now catches it.

**Spec reading to be aware of:** one `boundary-6` test expects `https://example.com/team/repo/.git` to become `…/repo//commit/<sha>`, with a double slash. That follows from the spec's order: trailing slashes are removed first, then one `.git`, and slashes are not removed again.

51 tests, in 2 files.

